From e8985f569bceddf60dd32909b72fd57489d51264 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 24 Jun 2026 19:59:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] patch 0255 -- gus fidelity (lever c): per-note multisample
 residency

The GUS backend (patch 0245) uploaded ONLY the single .pat sample nearest
middle C per instrument and pitch-shifted it across the whole keyboard. A
GM .pat is MULTISAMPLED -- the layer holds N samples, each rooted at a
different pitch and covering a key range -- so notes far from that one root
shift by octaves: the audible "off" timbre vs Gravis MIDIDEMO playing the
SAME bank.

Keep up to GUS_MAX_SUBSAMPLES (8) samples resident per instrument and pick
per-note at note_on. _upload_pat now parses every sample of the layer
(main-loop: file I/O + FPU ok), precomputes each sample's low_note/high_note
(from the .pat low_freq/high_freq fields) + root_note + fixed-point pitch
scaler, then uploads the selection ordered nearest-middle-C first. note_on
selects the resident subsample whose [low_note,high_note] covers the note,
else the nearest root -- integer-only, IRQ-safe (no file I/O / malloc / FPU
in the PIT-pump note path). DRAM is bounded by greedy fit: on the GF1's
~1MB filling, the upload stops and keeps the most central samples already
resident, degrading gracefully toward exactly the single 0245 sample --
never silence. Drums stay single-sample (a note selects the drum, it does
not transpose it). An always-on per-instrument summary logs N->K resident +
any K-cap / DRAM-truncation (no silent cap), and on_song_start reports the
per-song subsample total.

Gated by SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_GUS_MULTISAMPLE (cached once in the ctor per the
0250 pattern; bridged env->hint in main.cpp). Default ON; =0 reverts to the
single nearest-middle-C sample (patch 0245) for clean g2k A/B and the
low-DRAM SETUP off state. SETUP exposes it as the GUS_HIFI "GUS high
fidelity" config key (coordinated with setup-dev). Smoke banner
"gus backend: multisample ..." added (GUS-path only, optional).

